    rdf:value "D-1392" .

kg:note_ref24845_odd_0_p_1 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdf:value "FSA A.06 05.1392" .

kg:note_ref24845_odd_1_p_0 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdf:value "Transliteration on drawing paper related primarly to the excavation of Persepolis, initiated by Ernst Herzfeld in early March 1931, under the auspices of the Oriental Institute of the University of Chicago. Herzfeld left Persepolis permanently in Spring 1934." .

kg:note_ref24845_scopecontent_0_p_0 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdf:value "Additional information from Roland G. Kent's 1953 publication reads, \"DPf = Darius, Persepolis F: Elamite, 24 lines, on the south retaining wall of the palace.\"" .

kg:note_ref24845_scopecontent_1_p_0 a crm:E33_Linguistic_Object ;
    rdf:value "Additional information from Corpus Inscriptionum Iranicarum; Part I, Inscriptions of Ancient Iran; Vol. I, The Old Persian Inscriptions reads, \"The architectural activities in Persepolis started soon after 519 B.C. ...and continued until Alexander's arrival in 330 B.C.. The structures -and hence the cuneiform inscriptions- of Persepolis were thus created in the space of nearly two hundred years. The original approach to the Persepolis platform was from the south, but Xerxes blocked that entry when he finished the north-western stairway. Not far from the original southern stairway, Darius the Great had carved, on a single block, four cuneiform texts (DPd and DPe in Old Persian, DPf in Elamite, and DPg in Babylonian). These serve as the 'foundation inscriptions' of Persepolis.\" [Corpus Inscriptionum Iranicarum; Part I, Inscriptions of Ancient Iran; Vol. I, The Old Persian Inscriptions; Portfolio I: Plates i-xlviii. Old Persian Inscriptions of the Persepolis platform. Edited by A. Shapur Shahbazi. Published by Lund Humphries, London, 1985. Pp.15-16.\"]" .

