kg:component_ref9765 a crm:E73_Information_Object,
        mbs:ArchivalComponent,
        mbs:ItemUnit ;
    rdfs:label "D-1103: Samarra, Djausaq. Isometric reconstruction, marked \"VII Series: Samarra\"" ;
    dct:identifier "FSA A.06 05.1103" ;
    crm:P102_has_title kg:title_ref9765 ;
    crm:P129_is_about kg:concept_aat_300263552,
        kg:concept_architectural_drawing,
        kg:concept_art_of_the_islamic_world,
        kg:concept_excavations_archaeology,
        kg:concept_lcsh_sh85000061 ;
    crm:P1_is_identified_by kg:title_ref9765 ;
    crm:P2_has_type aat:300033973,
        kg:concept_drawings ;
    crm:P3_has_note "Arrangement Drawings are arranged roughly in sequential number sequences, housed in document boxes or in flat file folders by size, and stored in the map case drawers.",
        "Bibliography Leisten, Tomas, 2003: \"Excavation of Samarra, v. I. Architecture : Final report of the first campaign 1910-1912. Verlag Philipp von Zabern, Mainz am Rhein, 2003; p.87, fig.51.\"",
        "Date/Time and Place of an Event Note Drawings related primarly to the first campaign of excavation at Sāmarrāʼ (Iraq), carried out by Ernst Herzfeld on behalf of the Kaiser Friedrich Museum in Berlin in 1911.",
        "General - Title is provided by Xavier Courouble, FSg Archives cataloger, based on Thomas Leisten's publication, \"Excavation of Samarra, vol 1.\"",
        "Local Numbers D-1103 FSA A.06 05.1103",
        "Scope and Contents - Additional information reads, \"During the relatively short period of work at Manqūr / Balkuwārā in the summer and early fall of 1911, Herzfeld's efforts focused mainly on the central reception block between the third courtyard and the so-called river garden above the Tigris, especially on the eastern Īwān and some adjacent easterly rooms. He surveyed the rest of the reception block of the palace, including the cruciform domed hall in the center as well as the courtyard houses and ancillary complexes with the two maidāns to the south and north and added their general outlines, as far as they were visible among the ruins, to the plan. Herzfeld was certainly interested in the main architectonic features of the palace, but at the same time had hoped to uncover new varieties of stucco wall ornaments and to make major ceramic finds. In both he was disappointed. This probably contributed to his decision to stop the work on the site at the end of September 1911.\" [Leisten, Tomas, 2003: \"Excavation of Samarra, v. I. Architecture : Final report of the first campaign 1910-1912. Verlag Philipp von Zabern, Mainz am Rhein, 2003. p.91.\"]",
        "Scope and Contents - Handwritten annotations, probably added by Joseph Upton, on drawing reads, \"D-1103.\"",
        "Scope and Contents - Handwritten annotations, probably intended for publication, on drawing reads, \"bottom right corner: VII Series. Samarra; top right corner: b'.\"",
        "Scope and Contents - Handwritten date and initials on drawing reads, \"T. C. B.; 1919.\"",
        "Scope and Contents Excavation of Samarra (Iraq): Balkuwara Palace, Cruciform Reception-Hall Block and Courtyard III: Isometric Reconstruction [drawing]" ;
    crm:P48_has_preferred_identifier kg:id_ark_ref9765 ;
    crm:P67_refers_to kg:agent_Q62933,
        kg:note_ref9765_arrangement_0,
        kg:note_ref9765_bibliography_0,
        kg:note_ref9765_odd_0,
        kg:note_ref9765_odd_1,
        kg:note_ref9765_odd_2,
        kg:note_ref9765_scopecontent_0,
        kg:note_ref9765_scopecontent_1,
